# AegisLab · Grand Finale talk tracks (3-minute, 6-minute and 10-minute)

Team Tech Mavericks · Hack in Hills '26 · Track 04 · Grand Finale, Manali, 20–21 Nov 2026.

- **Code presented:** `main`, with finale P1–P5 merged on 12 Nov 2026. The screenshots and terminal recordings were made on commit `9145592`. The hosted read-only demo runs on Render from `main`.
- **Figures:** every figure below comes from `finale_package/claims_used_for_finale.json` and carries its type: **LIVE** (the presented code produces it when it runs; the number is the one from the rehearsal on 12 Nov, and the stage run must reproduce it), **RE-MEASURED <date>** (measured again on the finale code) or **RECORDED <date>** (measured earlier, not re-run). `aegislab/evaluation/check_finale_presentation.py` fails if a figure here differs from that file or lacks its label.
- **Format:** no file in `docs/round2/` or `round2/` records the finale format (`docs/round2/REQUIREMENTS_LOG.md` gives only the dates and the venue). The main track assumes a 6-minute pitch with a live demo, then judge Q&A. The 3-minute and 10-minute tracks reuse the same slides.
- **Never show:** the console launch link, API keys, `/home/...` paths, browser profile names. The launch link is opened off-screen before the talk.

Cue legend: **[SLIDE n]** change slide · **[DEMO]** switch to the terminal or browser · **[FALLBACK]** what to say and show if the live step fails · *(stage note, not spoken)*.

## Before going on stage (offline, about 10 minutes before)

1. `cd HIH/aegislab && bash scripts/real_model_demo.sh`: must end with {{real_model_demo.checks+}}. If not, use the fallbacks below for every real-model step.
2. In a second terminal: `bash scripts/start_real_model_console.sh`. Open the printed link in the browser **with the projector off or the window hidden**, then close the terminal tab that shows it. The browser lands on the Lab page with the header "REAL MODEL · LIVE".
3. Open the walkthrough video `AegisLab_Finale_Walkthrough.mp4` and the judge cut `AegisLab_Finale_Judge_Cut.mp4` in a player, paused at 0:00, on the same laptop (no network needed).
4. Clear the terminal (`clear`), set a large font, and hide the shell history.
5. Phone: open https://aegislab-demo.onrender.com if the venue has network; otherwise skip that step. Open it a few minutes early: on Render's free plan it sleeps after 15 minutes idle and takes about a minute to wake.

## 6-minute track (main)

| Time | Cue | Spoken (approximate; keep the figures and their labels exact) |
|---|---|---|
| 0:00–0:25 | **[SLIDE 1]** | "We are Tech Mavericks, and this is AegisLab. In an AI safety test a red team attacks, a blue team defends and the model answers, on one system. If red can read the defence, or one session can read another's data through the model, or a result is edited afterwards, the score measures the leak, not the model. Isolation decides whether the evaluation is valid." |
| 0:25–0:50 | **[SLIDE 2]** | "Six flows are forbidden, and each one is attacked in a test. The role matrix alone made {{role_matrix.attempts}} with {{role_matrix.denied}} ({{role_matrix.label}})." |
| 0:50–1:25 | **[SLIDE 3]** | "Five layers: rootless containers with no network, one trusted broker, a role gate, stateless model calls, and a signed audit chain. The real model runs in its own container with no network, reached through a UNIX socket. New for the finale: every container runs as {{f4_userns.inside}} on its own host UID, never as the broker's {{f4_userns.broker}}; a mounted key gives {{f4_userns.errno}} ({{f4_userns.label}})." |
| 1:25–1:35 | **[SLIDE 4]** | "Here is what you will see live, and what is recorded if the venue fails us." |
| 1:35–2:10 | **[DEMO]** terminal | Type `bash scripts/real_model_demo.sh`. While it runs: "This is the real model, now: stateless sessions, a control that must leak, and a leak the model makes through a broken gateway, withheld by our broker, even in base64." When it ends: "{{real_model_demo.checks}} in {{real_model_demo.secs}} (LIVE)." |
| 2:10–2:50 | **[DEMO]** browser, Lab | Create run (default policy) → Freeze → Start. "Default policy with the real model: deadline release." When it seals, point at the run card: "REAL MODEL · LIVE, deadline release at {{deadline_default.D}} (LIVE)." Run the authorised-release scenario. |
| 2:50–3:20 | **[DEMO]** browser, Evidence | Verify LAB-001: "Accepted by the independent verifier." Tamper with a copy: "One edited entry: rejected, original byte-identical." |
| 3:20–3:50 | **[SLIDE 5]** | "Real model, re-measured on the finale code: {{e1_isolation.sessions}} and {{e1_isolation.trials}} leaked ({{e1_isolation.label}}), while the controls built to leak did: {{e1_positive.history}} and {{e1_positive.gateway}} ({{e1_positive.label}}). No leak detected is not no leak: the upper bound is {{e1_bound.sessions}} ({{e1_bound.label}})." |
| 3:50–4:35 | **[SLIDE 6]** | "The result that changed. With the stub, 250 ms padding works: {{timing_stub.acc250}} ({{timing_stub.label}}). Against the real model it broke: {{timing_p3_single.acc250}} ({{timing_p3_single.label}}). Our own test found that. Deadline release fixed it: {{timing_deadline_real.acc}} against {{timing_deadline_real.base}} ({{timing_deadline_real.label}}). The third execution's {{timing_deadline_real.p3}} is below 0.05 but above our pre-declared {{timing_deadline_real.alpha}} ({{timing_deadline_real.label}}), so we say 'no leak detected', not 'no leak'. It costs {{timing_deadline_cost.cost}} per answer ({{timing_deadline_cost.label}})." |
